#!/usr/bin/python3
"""hearth-home-server-helper: the root half of Settings › Home server (docs/HOME-SERVER.md). Run by the Mantel helper
hearth-home-server through pkexec; /usr/share/polkit-1/rules.d/49-hearth-storage.rules lets the person signed in at the
device (local, active session, in the wheel group) run it without a password prompt. The snapshot timer runs
`snapshot run` as root.

It builds on Hearth Storage's root helper (hearth-storage-helper, next to this file): the same rules for which
folders may be shared, the same Samba service and password handling, and one Samba config for both (Hearth
Storage's shares in /etc/samba/hearth-storage.conf, the home server's in /etc/samba/hearth-home-server.conf).

  apply                 JSON on stdin (built from ~/.config/nimbus/home-server.json by hearth-home-server):
                        {"shares": [{"id","name","path","write","reach","recycle","snapshots","people"}], "tsOnly", "tsBoot",
                         "snapshots", "keep": {"hourly","daily","weekly"}, "lightMode", "people": [{"name","folder"}],
                         "backups": [{"id","name","paths","target","every","at","day","keep","on"}], "receive",
                         "alerts", "power"}
                        Samba's shares (SMB3 or newer, no guests, only you), "Tailscale only" firewall rules,
                        tailscaled at boot, the snapshot timer, /etc/hearth/home-server.json for the timer.
                        Samba runs only while at least one share is on. Every service here starts disabled.
  smb-password          your Samba password, one line on stdin (straight to smbpasswd, never kept or logged)
  drives                every drive: size, model, space, SMART health and temperature (smartctl), which is the system's
  format-plan DEV       what formatting DEV would do, the drive's name and size to confirm, and its token
  format                JSON on stdin {"device","use": "storage"|"backup"|"mirror","label","token","confirm1": true,
                        "confirm2": "<the drive's name · size, as format-plan gave it>","target" (mirror),"dryRun"}
  snapshot run          the timer: take the snapshots that are due and drop the ones past their keep counts
                        (waits while you play when Light mode is on)
  tailscale start|stop  tailscaled now (not at boot), and you as its operator so Settings can sign in and change it
  exit-node on|off      IP forwarding for "Let other devices use this PC's internet" (off unless you turn it on)
  wol IFACE             the network card wakes the PC from the network (Hearth Storage's helper does it)
  person-add            JSON on stdin {"name","password"}: a Samba-only account (a system account with no home, no login
                        shell and a locked system password, hidden from the login screen); the password only to smbpasswd
  person-password       JSON on stdin {"name","password"}
  person-remove NAME    the account and its sharing password go (only accounts Hearth made); nothing of theirs is deleted
  person-folder         JSON on stdin {"name","base","quotaGb"}: BASE/People/NAME (a btrfs subvolume, so it can have a quota)
  backup-secret ID      JSON on stdin {"password", "rest": {"user","password"}, "cloud": {"kind": "b2"|"s3", ...}}: written
                        0600 for root only (/etc/hearth/backup, rclone's config); never in your settings
  backup-forget ID      a removed job's password and credentials go (the backup itself is kept where it is)
  backup run            the timer: each backup that's due (restic), then old ones forgotten beyond the keep counts
  backup-now ID         that backup now, in its own transient unit (still waits while you play with Light mode on)
  backup-snapshots ID   its backups (restic snapshots)
  backup-restore        JSON on stdin {"id","snapshot","path"}: into a NEW folder in Home › Restored from backups, never over
  receive-add NAME      a backup code for one of your other Hearth devices (shown once; only its hash is kept)
  receive-remove USER
  alerts run|test       the alert timer: drive health, a drive filling up, a failed backup, a mirror missing a drive
  power run             the power timer: spin down idle data drives, stay awake while used, sleep when nobody needs it
  ups-status            the UPS's charge (upsc)

The user is always the one who ran pkexec (PKEXEC_UID), never an argument. Nothing here goes through a shell: every
command is an argument list. Paths and names are checked here again (the Mantel helper checks them first): a share
is a folder inside your Home (not Home itself) or on a drive mounted under /run/media/YOU, /media/YOU, /mnt or
/var/mnt that isn't the system drive; never /, /etc, /usr, /boot, /sysroot, /ostree, nothing hidden on the way.
One JSON line on stdout: {"ok": true, ...} or {"ok": false, "error": "..."}.
"""
import hashlib
import importlib.machinery
import importlib.util
import json
import os
import pwd
import re
import shutil
import subprocess
import sys
